On 22 Jun 2006 13:26:07 -0700, ioququ@googlemail.com wrote:

> > Or change the system - point out and lobby for perhaps better ways
> > that nethack can be updated and maintained.
> 
> You're going to have to do it yourself - I see a handful of independent
> patchwriters, not a slavering horde of developers eager to take over
> the reigns of power.


I'd wager that the major patch writers and interface maintainers have
done more work on nethack, by lines of code and by time, than the
devteam has since the last release.  We can't know for sure because
the devteam hasn't *released* anything in years to look at.  But it's
not as if the nethack project requires any full-time developers (I'm
sure the devteam people have day jobs), certainly not any more time
than the hours put in by these unofficial contributors, who actually
produce things every month or 6 months or year.  If these people's
time were coordinated well, we'd have more frequent nethack releases
with more interesting, balanced new features.

 
> Besides, we play NetHack at least partly because of the mystique the
> DevTeam's secrecy has earned it. As an analogy, Star Trek fan-fiction
> may be better than the real thing (and available: there's been no new
> Trek for years either), but it's *not* the real thing.


You might.  I play it because it's a good game, in spite of the
irritating development secrecy and snail pace - there're glaciers in
Greenland retreating faster than inches of official code coming out.
Neat ideas embodied in some of the patches fall by the wayside, and I
suspect for other people also, because of the fussiness of trying to
get them to work with each other - again because of the lack of
coordination and the chaos which fills these giant delays between
official releases.

